data: {"run_id":"84f591fdbe2e442e82ea88b979a4f03f","seq":10,"stage":"respond","status":"ok","parent":null,"label":"10","data":{"action":"answer","system":"You are a career agent inside a job-search product. Answer the user's career question helpfully and concretely. Keep the answer under 200 words.","max_tokens":300,"max_chars":1500,"provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-4-5","text":"**No, you're not obligated to share your current salary.**\n\nHere's what to consider:\n\n**Why you might decline:**\n- Salary anchoring: Sharing a lower number can cap your offer\n- Privacy: It's personal financial information\n- Negotiation leverage: Keeping it private strengthens your position\n\n**How to decline professionally:**\n- \"I prefer to discuss the salary range for this role instead\"\n- \"I'd rather focus on what you're offering for this position\"\n- \"My compensation has varied with bonuses—can we discuss the range you have budgeted?\"\n\n**When it might matter:**\n- Some states (CA, NY, IL, etc.) now prohibit asking for salary history\n- If they truly need it for compliance, they'll explain why\n- You can offer a range of what you're seeking instead\n\n**Better approach:**\nResearch the role's market rate and share your **salary expectations** for the new position, rather than your current salary. This keeps the conversation forward-focused and fair.\n\nMost experienced recruiters expect candidates to deflect this question—it's standard. A good one will move on and discuss the role's budget instead.","truncated":false,"recorded_at":"2026-10-29T14:35:17+00:00","replayed":true},"ms":3293,"tokens":313,"cost_usd":0.001337}

